\import{macros}
\import{lean-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}

\taxon{Definition}
\title{Framed transports}

\p{Let #{I} be a finite set of positions, #{\Omega} a nonempty set,
and #{K} a group acting faithfully on #{\Omega}. At each position #{i},
choose a set #{X_i} of local labels and a bijective frame
#{c_i:\Omega\to X_i}. Keep the fibers distinct by using the tagged union}
##{E=\coprod_{i\in I}X_i=\{(i,y):i\in I,\ y\in X_i\}.}
\p{A \em{framed transport} is a permutation of #{E} given by a position
permutation #{\pi\in\operatorname{Sym}(I)} and elements #{a_i\in K}, with}
##{g(i,c_i(x))=(\pi(i),c_{\pi(i)}(a_i x)).}
\p{Write #{g=(\pi,a)}. Its local factors are indexed by the source
position. Nonempty fibers determine #{\pi} uniquely, and faithfulness
determines each #{a_i} uniquely. Conversely every such pair gives a
permutation: in framed coordinates its inverse sends
#{(j,x)} to #{(\pi^{-1}(j),a_{\pi^{-1}(j)}^{-1}x)}.}

\p{This finite-position model isolates the coordinate mechanism of
\citet{Section 3.1, Definition 7 and equation (5)}{marchetti2025rubik}.
Which local label sets and local group describe a particular puzzle must
still be justified for that puzzle. The requirement that a transport moves
whole fibers is part of the model.}

\p{Under composition these transports form
#{K\wr\operatorname{Sym}(I)}. The normal subgroup #{K^I} acts within
fibers, and position permutations reorder its factors. In
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.WreathProduct}, a pair is stored using the
destination-indexed function #{A_j=a_{\pi^{-1}(j)}}. Its permutation
action is \leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.WreathProduct.imprimitiveToPerm_apply};
the nonempty-fiber and faithful-action hypotheses give its injectivity in
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.WreathProduct.imprimitiveToPerm_injective}.
The bijections #{c_i} identify that action on #{I\times\Omega} with
the action on #{E} above.}
